AUDIO: 'This may just look like an ordinary sticker, but what if I told you this sticker [music] can actually help transform your life and help you stick to better routines? And it costs only 35. It sounds too good to be true, I know, but I assure you it\'s not. So, today you and I are going to dive into the world of NFC tags. I\'m going to show'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you some of my favorite [music] ways to use these to automate my life, to add in efficiencies, to help me make better choices. There\'s a little bit of programming involved, but I\'m going to help you along every step of the way. So, don\'t be too intimidated. Without further ado, let\'s dive in. What is an'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'NFC tag anyway? Well, NFC stands for near field communication. And within each NFC is a built-in microchip, really tiny one that you can program so that when it\'s scanned, your your tag does'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'things. And also an antenna, which allows your NFC tag to communicate wirelessly with NFC readers so that it can read your chip and then perform the action. So, our phones are a good example of something that is able to'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'read an NFC tag because most modern smartphones have wireless payment options, and that is the technology that we are using to program our little chips. I buy my NFC tags on Amazon. These ones are in sticker form, so I can'

[01:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually peel them and stick them anywhere, but you can also sometimes find them in like these little chips that don\'t necessarily need to be pasted anywhere. I mean, even if I don\'t want to put a sticker on something, I just'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like will cut out the NFC tag with the sticker backing and place it wherever I want. So, I feel like the sticker is a good way to have like as many options as possible. This right here was originally a 30 pack. I paid $9.99 Canadian for it,'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so very inexpensive and I feel like you can even find them for cheaper if you\'re planning to buy like 50 or 100. Now, this first NFC use case is actually the major inspo behind this video because'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when I shared it, I had a lot of people ask me about NFC tags. And I don\'t blame you because this is one of my favorite ways to use NFC tags in 2026. And is to create your own DIY brick. Now, how the brick works is essentially you buy this'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'device that you stick on as a magnet on your fridge. And whenever you want to lock yourself out of distracting apps like social media, you tap your phone against it. It locks your apps. And to unlock them, you have to go back to your'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fridge, unlock your brick, and then you will be able to access those apps again. Essentially, what it\'s creating is like this physical barrier of having to get up from your desk, get up from your bed, get up from your couch to intentionally unlock those apps on your phone and then'

[02:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use them rather than allowing yourself to get into this autopilot mode of scrolling. I love the concept so much, I decided to investigate how to make it for myself and ended up creating my own brick. This is a little marble holder'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that I have in my entryway. So, it\'s not on my fridge. And on the lid, you\'ll see I have an NFC sticker. And when I want to lock the social media apps on my phone, I scan it. And all of a sudden, all the social media apps can\'t be'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'opened. If I try and open Instagram, this is what I see. Tik Tok. I DIYed my brick in an app called Focus. It is a free app in case you\'re wondering. I\'ll have a link to it down in the description box. It\'s so easy to set up. It allows you to customize which apps'

[03:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you want to lock down because maybe the apps that are distracting to me are different than the apps that are distracting to you. I\'ve been using it for over a month now and some of the reasons I really like it is because it keeps me off those distracting apps'

[03:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'while still giving me access to my phone which I do need. I still need to use the camera app and answer emails and text messages and do all other workrelated tasks like social media is my job. So there is a lot of stuff that needs to'

[03:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'happen on here even editing videos or pictures. But then it also pauses the autopilot scrolling, which like I like scrolling, but I don\'t want to always be scrolling or be doing it in a mind-numbing way. So, I think there\'s a balance to be had there. Just the'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tension of seeing that I\'ve already locked the apps makes me realize that, oh my goodness, I\'m like so frequently just going to these apps all the time just to fill up seconds in my day that'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are filled with like nothing. And I think we all can embrace a little bit more nothingness and pause and quiet and non-distraction time, but I I will admit it\'s it\'s such a hard thing to embrace because I think we\'ve become accustomed'

